How the data was analyzed.

\section{Results}
This section presents the results of the study.

\section{Conclusion}
This is the conclusion section. It summarizes the findings and provides future directions.

\end{document}
```

### Explanation:
1. **`\documentclass{article}`**: Specifies the type of document (in this case, an article). Other document classes like `report` or `book` can also be used.
2. **`\tableofcontents`**: This command generates the table of contents. It should be placed where you want the table of contents to appear in the document.
3. **`\section`, `\subsection`, and `\subsubsection`**: These commands are used to create sections, subsections, and sub-subsections, respectively. The labels created by these commands can be referenced using the `\label` command, which is useful for cross-referencing.
4. **`\label{sec:background}`**: This creates a label for the "Background" section, which can be referenced in the document using `\ref{sec:background}`.

When you compile this LaTeX document, it will generate a table of contents that lists all the sections and subsections along with their page numbers.

